 ### 7. Large Project Count and Graph Shape
 
 - **Symptoms**: many small projects, each takes minimal time but overhead adds up; deep dependency chains serialize the build
 - **Consider**: project consolidation, or use `/graph` mode for better scheduling
 - **Graph shape matters**: a wide dependency graph (few levels, many parallel branches) builds faster than a deep one (many levels, serialized). Refactoring from deep to wide can yield significant improvements in both clean and incremental build times.
 - **Actions**: look for unnecessary project dependencies, consider splitting a bottleneck project into two, or merging small leaf projects
 
+### 8. ResolveProjectReferences Looks Expensive but Is Not the Bottleneck
+
+- **Symptom**: `ResolveProjectReferences` tops the **Target** Performance Summary, sometimes at 50-80% of build time
+- **Why it misleads**: the reported time is wall-clock **wait time for dependent projects** while the MSBuild node is yielded (dotnet/msbuild#3135) — the node may be doing useful work elsewhere; the target itself does very little
+- **Do not** set `ResolveProjectReferences` as the optimization target
+- **Instead**: read the **Task** Performance Summary and optimize task self-time — `Csc` (section 2), `ResolveAssemblyReference` (section 1), `Copy` (section 4); serialization between projects is covered by the `build-parallelism` skill
+
